-- SANGEN: lobby-composition-driven playable area, spawn positions, per-army mex
-- (alloy) visibility, and (4 human players only) a per-army naval fleet.
--
-- Loaded automatically by the game's own STOCK, UNMODIFIED map-data mechanism:
-- common/mapUtils.lua's LoadMapData() checks for "maps/<mapName>/<mapName>_data.lua"
-- next to every map's .sanmap and Import()s it if present. Not a custom hook -- it's
-- vanilla game code, so it survives a game patch.
--
-- Map-folder file only. Lives at LJ/lua/maps/Pandemonium Isthmus/ (NOT the
-- Sanctuary_Data/Maps folder -- that's where the .sanmap/Props/Textures live; libPath,
-- which this path is built from, points at LJ/lua instead).
--
-- ORCHESTRATOR ONLY, 2026-08-20: per the scenario-script file split
-- (sangen_arch_pack/specs/MODDING_SCRIPTING_SPEC.md, "Scenario-script file split"),
-- this file holds no scenario content of its own -- every scenario definition, the
-- area/alloy/spawn resolution, and the naval fleet spawner live in the sibling
-- Pandemonium Isthmus_Scenarios_Script.lua. This file's job is strictly: read the
-- lobby, determine the slot pattern, hand both to the scenario module, then wire the
-- result into the map's playable area / naval spawn.
--
-- The two files are COLOCATED here in LJ/lua/maps/<MapName>/, and must stay that way.
-- Putting the scenario file in the map's asset folder was tried on 2026-08-20 and
-- PROVEN IMPOSSIBLE: Import() cannot address anything outside the LJ/lua root -- a
-- "../../Sanctuary_Data/..." path fails Engine.FileExists() outright (verified live
-- in-game against the exact libPath.."/"..path string import.lua builds internally),
-- so Import() raises "File doesn't exist" and aborts this whole chunk. That settles
-- Sanctuary_Map_System_Rework.md Sec12 open question 6 in the NEGATIVE. If this file
-- ever relocates to the map folder, the scenario file moves with it.
--
-- Import() (common/systems/import.lua) does NOT use a module's `return` statement; it
-- only captures GLOBAL variables the file writes into its own environment table.
-- Scenarios_Script.lua exposes its API as a global `Scenario` table, so this file
-- pulls `.Scenario` off the Import() result below -- `local S = Import(path)` alone
-- silently yields a table with none of our fields and no error.
--
-- IMPORTANT -- everything below runs inside LoadMapData(), which nothing up the call
-- chain protects with pcall (Import()'s own xpcall catches internal errors but
-- RE-RAISES after logging). An uncaught error here aborts LoadMapData entirely, which
-- means CreateArmies() and RunMapSetup() never run afterward: no armies, no units, no
-- props, no markers, no resource spots -- confirmed by a live crash on 2026-08-16.
-- The whole body is wrapped in pcall with a safe fallback (map loads at its normal
-- defaults) so this file can never take the rest of map load down with it again.


-- ============================================================================
-- LOAD-SCOPE GATE -- required. Do not remove.
--
-- This file has TWO callers, and only ONE of them is asking to start a match:
--
--   common/mapUtils.lua:47   LoadMapData()          Import("maps/X/X_data.lua")
--       The real thing. Runs during InitLobby, before CreateArmies. Owns gameplay setup.
--
--   host/testUtils.lua:364   LoadDebugUnitGroups()  Import("/maps/X/X_data.lua")
--       Called unconditionally from host/hostMain.lua:23. Wants ONE thing: the `MapData`
--       global's .groups table, to populate the debug unit-group UI. It is not starting a
--       match and has no business running playable-area, alloy, or unit-spawn logic.
--
-- Import() caches on the LITERAL string it is given (common/systems/import.lua:128), and
-- "maps/..." != "/maps/...", so the second call misses the cache and RE-EXECUTES this whole
-- chunk in the same Lua state. Measured 2026-08-28: 300 fighters and 40 ships against the 150
-- and 20 configured, and two NewThread registrations. Commanders were never doubled because
-- CreateArmies is not in this chunk -- which is what made the symptom look selective.
--
-- The gate below is NOT a "run once" counter. It is this file correctly scoping its side
-- effects to the caller that owns them, using the path Import itself recorded
-- (ImportedFileInfo.FileName, set at import.lua:177). The debug loader still gets a fully
-- successful import and still reads whatever data globals it wants -- it simply does not
-- trigger match setup. That is the correct division of responsibility, and it stays correct
-- if the engine ever normalizes its cache key.
--
-- BACKGROUND -- why gameplay logic lives in a data file at all: this engine no longer has a
-- per-map script hook. `<map>_script.lua` (referenced by Pandemonium Isthmus_SpawnArmies.lua.bak
-- as its caller) does not exist any more, and the only surviving per-map script,
-- maps/showcase_script.lua, is invoked by hand from a debug command (host/testUtils.lua:544),
-- not automatically. `<map>_data.lua` is the ONLY automatic per-map Lua entry point left, so
-- match logic has nowhere else to go.
--
-- ⚠️ RELATED HAZARD, not solved here: host/testUtils.lua:353 does
--     table.save(DebugUnitGroups, DebugUnitGroupsFilePath, "MapData = ")
-- i.e. the engine can OVERWRITE this file with machine-generated data if the debug
-- unit-group UI is used. Everything in this file would be destroyed. Keep a backup.
local sangenLoadPath = ImportedFileInfo and ImportedFileInfo.FileName or ""
if sangenLoadPath ~= string.format("maps/%s/%s_data.lua",
        GameInfo.MapInfo.dataName, GameInfo.MapInfo.dataName) then
    return {}
end
-- ============================================================================

local ok, err = pcall(function()

    local Scenario = Import("maps/Pandemonium Isthmus/Pandemonium Isthmus_Scenarios_Script.lua").Scenario

    -- SANGEN, 2026-08-27: air/land/sea/amphib/hover/sub navmesh blocker over the two spire
    -- footprints near map center. Reuses the existing PlayableAreaBarrier prefab (common/
    -- systems/templateLoader.lua creates it once per map, on both host and client, for every
    -- map, well before this file runs) rather than defining a new prefab -- it already carries
    -- one NavmapModifierTemplate child per navigation layer, for EVERY layer including Air
    -- (common/loading/playableAreaBarrierLoader.lua:19-26, Layers.GetAllNavigationLayerNames()).
    -- A plain structure's skirtSize modifiers explicitly exclude Air
    -- (common/layers.lua:144-146, GetStructureNavigationLayerNames), so reusing THIS prefab
    -- specifically -- not a unit template's skirtSize -- is what makes Air blocking possible
    -- here at all, with zero engine/mapUtils.lua changes.
    --
    -- The map's real heightmap is flat under both spires (the visual spires are props, not
    -- terrain) -- irrelevant here: a NavmapModifier blocks its footprint independent of local
    -- terrain height/slope (common/navigationLayers.lua's per-layer height/slope bands only
    -- gate the terrain-driven bake, not an explicit modifier), and the barrier prefab's own Y
    -- is always 0 regardless of terrain (common/playableAreaBarrier.lua's GetBarrierSlabs).
    --
    -- Rectangles decomposed from Textures/heightmap_AirBlocker.png (2049x2049, matches this
    -- map's heightmapResolution). History: human edited this mask three times (2026-08-27) --
    -- first pass covered only the 2 spires nearest map center (93-94% coverage, few boxes);
    -- second pass covered all 32 real white regions across the whole map at a tight 16px
    -- merge-overshoot cap (815 rectangles, 0 missed white, ~3.5-15% overshoot per component);
    -- this THIRD pass re-scans the human's latest mask edit and re-runs the same pipeline at a
    -- much looser 512px merge-overshoot cap, per the human's explicit ask this time: prefer
    -- covering extra black area over adding more rectangles.
    --
    -- Full scan: thresholded at raw16 > 32768, connected-component-labeled across the whole
    -- mask. 32 real components survive a size>=20px cutoff (16 unique features, each mirrored
    -- once -- this map is point-symmetric); sub-20px specks are antialiasing dust, dropped.
    --
    -- Per component: an EXACT (zero-overshoot) greedy largest-rectangle decomposition first
    -- (100% coverage by construction), then the same greedy agglomerative merge pass as before --
    -- repeatedly replace the two rectangles whose union bounding box adds the fewest net-new
    -- black pixels (integral image over that component's true mask, overlap-safe) with that one
    -- bounding box -- but stopping only once the cheapest remaining merge would add more than
    -- 512 black px (vs. 16 previously). Merging only ever grows coverage, so zero-missed-white
    -- still holds for all 32 components (confirmed via full union-vs-mask rasterization).
    --
    -- Totals at this setting: 139 rectangles across all 32 components (vs. 815 at the 16px cap --
    -- an 83% cut), total overshoot 30901/74482 white px = 41.5% (vs. ~5% at the 16px cap) -- the
    -- explicit trade the human asked for. Reference curve computed before picking 512: 256px cap
    -- -> 206 rects/29.3% overshoot; 1024px cap -> 88 rects/58.8% overshoot (already more black
    -- than white in the average box -- judged too aggressive). No rotation:
    -- NavmapModifierTemplate's `size` is an axis-aligned float2, so every diagonal blob is
    -- staircase-approximated, now with noticeably blockier, over-sized steps at this cap.
    --
    -- Pixel -> world: x = 2048 - col, z = row (heightmapResolution-1 = 2048 = width = length).
    -- This is the SANMAP_FORMAT_SPEC-documented, empirically-validated (median error 0.0105
    -- world units over 63k instances) heightmap-sampling convention -- chosen over the format's
    -- separate general-entity-position flip convention because this mask is a Textures/ raster
    -- asset in the same family as heightmap.raw, not a `.sanmap` JSON entity field. Cross-checked
    -- 2026-08-27 against this map's own 164 real Spire_01/CliffSpireCap_0[1-3] prop positions for
    -- the two center components only (the map's only "spire"-named props): 158/164 (96%) fell
    -- within a 20px-dilated first-pass mask under this convention, confirmed live in-game the
    -- same day. Not re-validated against this mask edit or the other 30 components.
